8|0@Israel^1440503286#cueyjlq:KingJewffrey$:Personally I whined throughout my entire service. Started whining right after bootcamp and didn't stop till I was demobilized 3 years later. Mandatory conscription is like a Russian roulette, you have little choice where you will serve and who will be your commanders. Some people remember their service as a positive experience, maybe because they had a good commander and made some good friends. My commander was an insufferable incompetent douchebag, and thanks to him I hated every minute of my service. In a mandatory army, your superiors aren't people who are smarter/more talented/better educated/more experienced than you, they are just young people who happen to join the army a year before you did, and thus one can easily finds himself in my situation, in which his commander is a borderline retarded asshole who doesn't know what he's doing half of the time, but he has the means to turn your life into a living hell, and in case of a war, you are expected to follow him into battle and most likely die out of his stupidity. So yeah, wasted 3 years of my life for nothing, if I could some how go back and skip that shit I would in an instant. If you think the military is good for you and that you might flourish in such an environment, then by all means join, but please don't force it on other people who might not feel the same as you. 
