2|0@Israel^1440489093#cuev3on:solatic$:The reason why people don't whine about conscription here is because we know a professional army wouldn't be big enough to actually defend us (at least, based on what we could afford with our current defense budget), and we know that if we didn't have a (meaningful, I.e. not European sized) army, then we'd all die tomorrow. When the IDF formed from the remnants of disbanded pre-state paramilitaries, the threats were Egypt, Jordan, Iraq, Syria, and Lebanon. Today, those threats are Hamas, Hezbollah, the Islamic State, and Iran. The enemies may change but as Jews, we'll always have people who hate us, and we all know we each have to step up and serve in our common defense. Maybe that's a feeling that can be found in Ukraine after Putin's invasion, but from what I read, Ukraine is too divided between people who see Russia as an enemy and Russia as a friend. In Israel, we have no such divisions - virtually nobody sees Hamas, Hezbollah, Islamic State, or Iran as their friends (except maybe some on the extremist fringe of the Arab-Israeli sector). If there's one universal truth here though, it's that the only way to really get people to do stuff they don't want to do is to pay them well for it. Conscription salaries are universally terrible, and in every conscription army you will find demoralized people dragging their feet. The only way in which the IDF differs in this regard is that conscripted soldiers overwhelmingly show up and do their job anyway because of the reasons outlined above. Conscription armies where conscription isn't seen as something making a difference in national survival - say, Switzerland (EU states are going to invade Switzerland in the near future? lolno), Taiwan (like a tiny Taiwanese military could ever stand up to the Chinese military without foreign help), even South Korea (a starving North Korean military would ever really take over the South? Especially against American troops stationed there?) - are armies with far worse social realities. Conscription armies do end up with better rich/poor integration than professional armies, but don't kid yourself - rich people still work the system. During the Vietnam War, the upper-class didn't get conscripted due (at least) to university exemptions. In Israel, if it's not greasing the palms of the doctors who write up medical exemptions, it's fake marriages (see: Bar Rafaeli) or, at the very least, helping their kids find their way into office jobs far from the front lines - often the kind of office job that leads to opportunities in the civilian world after the end of their service. There are some rich kids in combat units here but they're few and far between.
6|0@Israel^1440485433#cueu4p8:akolada$:I'm a strong military supporter. I think conscription is a good thing. I don't think its necessary for all countries, but the military offers much more than defense. It gives you education, training and career opportunities as well as teaching you many valuable life skills. I did not serve in Israel's military because I moved here when I was too old to join as a woman (I was 22 at that time), so they would not accept me. Its something I feel pretty bad about because I feel like I missed out on something that was important to me. When I hear about people dodging the army (and they don't have a legitimate health concern) I think extremely lowly of them and I do not keep them as friends because I think it reflects poorly on their character. Any children I may choose to have will serve in the military G-d willing, I know people who wait to immigrate to Israel until after their kids are grown so they avoid drafting and I do not have any respect for them. My husband was a commander in a combat unit so I will be very proud if my sons (or daughters) choose to take on a few extra years to do something similar. 
