18|0@Israel^1440452918#cuedhwr:pitaenigma$:First off, a lot of Israelis whine, and a lot don't go into the army or drop out for inconsequential reasons. We have a reputation as ridiculous badasses which is fun when I'm in Foreignia but is pretty far from the truth. I think that the IDF in its current form does a lot for Israel other than defence - It helps bridge economical gaps, giving the same advantages to the rich and the poor alike (not perfectly, but mostly). It creates unity in the people who go. My best friends in the army were actually born in your area, and we're from such different parts of the country that it would be mindboggling for us to become friends in any other way. It also straightens out a lot of people who are somewhat criminalish in their behavior - I know quite a few people whose lives were fucked up who managed to get them back on track partly because of the army's help. But the truth is that if I had had the choice at the time I probably wouldn't have enlisted. I believe that people should, but if it weren't a draft situation I probably wouldn't myself. Hypocrisy at its finest, but oh well.
3|0@Israel^1440526986#cufc21h:Chester_b$:I want to say thank you to every person who left a comment in this thread! Just don't want to thank everyone personally, so I'll leave it here under the top comment. It's really interesting for me, as Ukrainian whose country experiences really hard times, to hear your opinions, since your country, as I already said before, often used as an example here. I'd like to clarify some points, though. When here in Ukraine someone mentions Israel as an outstanding example of commitment and duty, people mostly take that part where "Everybody serves in Israel!" but ignore that part where military service offer people something in exchange, like social elevators, opportunities for a career start or figuring out what do you want to do with your life. In our case we have the following. We have a war on the east of our country against regular Russian army units and hybrid Russian units consisting of Russian mercenaries and local collaborators. During the last one and half of year since annexation of Crimea peninsula we have had six waves of mobilization, only men are subjects of this process, except very little percent women with military education and title. We gained more or less enough power to withstand the war in its current form but still not enough to fight against Russia if Putin decides to invade full scale. At the same time we call this war a war only among the regular people, while our authorities gave it official status "ATO" (Анти-Террористическая Операция or Counter-Terrorist Operation). So we don't have a war officially, we call Russia an aggressor, but we don't withdraw our diplomats from Russia, many of our oligarchs has business ties with Russia and even our president (who publicly calls Russia Mordor) has a factory there which still operates. This makes the goals of this war sometimes unclear and vague for many people to be honest. Our government almost failed at the fight against corruption and even though some positive changes in the country really do happen, it doesn't seem enough considering how big the price our nation paid on Maidan and how much bigger it has become after war started. But the most frustrating part for me is that even though now our army is pretty powerful, it still ruled by old "parade generals" who robbed the army for 23 years and in many aspects it works by the old soviet principles. We have many volunteer battalions ruled by Ministry of Internal Affairs, where the situation is a bit better but in general our armed forces are far from something you can call modern. 100% of thermographic cameras provided to our army by so called "civil volenteers" who collect money in Internet. We have thousands of people across the country who collect money to buy good equipment, theromgraphic cameras, night vision devices, transport vehicles in EU, modern sniper rifles in EU or USA, equipment for personal weapons, build drones for intelligence, buy normal food etc. etc. etc. and they deliver it to the very front line. You can't even imagine how much was done by common people for our armed forces. You can google for photos of Ukrainian-Russian war and find out that Ukrainian units have almost all kinds of uniforms, like German, British, American, Austrian, Ukrainian (thank god we have our uniform too) and many others. If not the common people and their initiative in the last year, we would be already defeated for sure no doubts. There are many cases when hardly injured fighter needs an operation and his relatives collect money to pay a surgery because our hospitals might have not enough materials\equipment\professionals\whatever for that. Hundreds of fighters can't get their documents approving they were in the war defending their country because of bureaucracy and negligence of higher ranks. Our nation spills its blood literally every day for more than one and half year but our government is not in a hurry to change what they must to change and they call the people on to not be cowards and go defend the country because we defend not them, but rather our land which is also true, but many of us find such kind of relations between the state and the people unfair. The last sixth wave of mobilization was accomplished for only 50% because people morale goes down seeing how our government behaves and what decisions it takes. That's why we have so many discussions on what our army should be, in what form and who must serve there. 
